<article class="env-lemma" id="0FXC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FXC">Lemma <span data-tag="0FXC">13.40.4</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{D}$ be a triangulated category. Let $\mathcal{A} \subset \mathcal{D}$ be a full subcategory invariant under all shifts. Then both the right orthogonal $\mathcal{A}^\perp $ and the left orthogonal ${}^\perp \mathcal{A}$ of $\mathcal{A}$ are strictly full, saturated<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000279772" id="a0000279772-mark"><sup>1</sup></a>, triangulated subcagories of $\mathcal{D}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It is immediate from the definitions that the orthogonals are preserved under taking shifts, direct sums, and direct summands. Consider a distinguished triangle 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  X \to Y \to Z \to X[1]  \]
</div>
    <p>
       of $\mathcal{D}$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/05QX" data-tag="05QX">13.4.16</a> it suffices to show that if $X$ and $Y$ are in $\mathcal{A}^\perp $, then $Z$ is in $\mathcal{A}^\perp $. This is immediate from Lemma <a href="/tag/0CQQ" data-tag="0CQQ">13.40.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>